{"records":[{"id":"nrgzzm973b","title":"The essential role of heparan sulfate in the entry of PDCoV and other porcine coronaviruses","description":"Figure 1. Heparin sodium treatment of cells inhibits PDCoV infection.\nFigure 2. Heparin sodium treatment inhibits infection by multiple coronaviruses. \nFigure 3. Mitoxantrone, an HS-binding drug, inhibits PDCoV infection.\nFigure 4. The effects of genetic ablation of HS biosynthesis genes on PDCoV infection. \nFigure 5. SLC35B2, EXT1 and NDST1 HS biosynthesis genes are required for efficient infection by PEDV, SADS-CoV and TGEV.\nFigure 6. SLC35B2, EXT1 and NDST1 gene KO in LLC-PK1 cells reduces PDCoV attachment.\nFigure 7. Impact of the overexpression of HS biosynthesis genes on porcine coronavirus infection. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-17T14:46:41.841Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nrgzzm973b","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nrgzzm973b"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ningning","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xvz5xs98wk","title":"The essential role of heparan sulfate in the entry of PDCoV and other porcine coronaviruses","description":"igure 1. Heparin sodium treatment of cells inhibits PDCoV infection.\nFigure 2. Heparin sodium treatment inhibits infection by multiple coronaviruses. \nFigure 3. Mitoxantrone, an HS-binding drug, inhibits PDCoV infection.\nFigure 4. The effects of genetic ablation of HS biosynthesis genes on PDCoV infection. \nFigure 5. SLC35B2, EXT1 and NDST1 HS biosynthesis genes are required for efficient infection by PEDV, SADS-CoV and TGEV.\nFigure 6. SLC35B2, EXT1 and NDST1 gene KO in LLC-PK1 cells reduces PDCoV attachment.\nFigure 7. Impact of the overexpression of HS biosynthesis genes on porcine coronavirus infection. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-12T19:53:57.87Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xvz5xs98wk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xvz5xs98wk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ningning","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32473007"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32272990"}],"categories":["Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9fg82mgf8z","title":"The essential role of heparan sulfate in the entry of PDCoV and other porcine coronaviruses","description":"Figure 1. The effect of heparin sodium treatment on PDCoV infection. \nFigure 2. Heparin sodium treatment inhibits infection by multiple coronaviruses. \nFigure 3. The effect of mitoxantrone treatment on PDCoV infection.\nFigure 4. The effects of genetic ablation of HS biosynthesis genes on PDCoV infection.\nFigure 5. SLC35B2, EXT1 and NDST1 HS biosynthesis genes are required for efficient infection by PEDV, SADS-CoV and TGEV.\nFigure 6. SLC35B2, EXT1 and NDST1 gene KO in LLC-PK1 cells reduces PDCoV attachment. \nFigure 7. Impact of the overexpression of HS biosynthesis genes on porcine coronavirus infection.\nFigure S1. Heparin sodium treatment showed no inhibitory effect on VSV infection.\nFigure S2. Mitoxantrone exhibits a stronger inhibitory effect on the viral entry stage than on post-entry stages.\nFigure S3. Effect of mitoxantrone treatment on PDCoV dsRNA formation.\nFigure S4. Mitoxantrone treatment inhibits infection by multiple coronaviruses.\nFigure S5. Mitoxantrone treatment of cells had no significant effect on VSV infection.\nFigure S6. Effect of genetic ablation of HS biosynthesis genes on HS expression.\nFigure S7. The effect of EXT1 overexpression on the expression of SLC35B2 and NDST1.\nFigure S8. Overexpression of HS biosynthesis genes had no significant effect on VSV infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-30T08:43:36.816Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9fg82mgf8z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9fg82mgf8z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ningning","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32272990"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32473007"}],"categories":["Virus","Animal Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4bx7pyjms8","title":"The essential role of heparan sulfate in the entry of PDCoV and other porcine coronaviruses","description":"Figure 1. The effect of heparin sodium treatment on PDCoV infection. \nFigure 2. Heparin sodium treatment inhibits infection by multiple coronaviruses. \nFigure 3. The effect of mitoxantrone treatment on PDCoV infection.\nFigure 4. The effects of genetic ablation of HS biosynthesis genes on PDCoV infection. \nFigure 5. SLC35B2, EXT1 and NDST1 HS biosynthesis genes are required for efficient infection by PEDV, SADS-CoV and TGEV.\nFigure 6. SLC35B2, EXT1 and NDST1 gene KO in LLC-PK1 cells reduces PDCoV attachment.\nFigure 7. Impact of the overexpression of HS biosynthesis genes on porcine coronavirus infection.\nFigure S1. Heparin sodium treatment showed no inhibitory effect on VSV infection.\nFigure S2. Mitoxantrone exhibits a stronger inhibitory effect on the viral entry stage than on post-entry stages.\nFigure S3. Effect of mitoxantrone treatment on PDCoV dsRNA formation. \nFigure S4. Mitoxantrone treatment inhibits infection by multiple coronaviruses. \nFigure S5. Mitoxantrone treatment of cells had no significant effect on VSV infection. \nFigure S6. Effect of genetic ablation of HS biosynthesis genes on HS expression. \nFigure S7. Overexpression of HS biosynthesis genes had no significant effect on VSV infection. \nFigure S8. The effect of EXT1 overexpression on the expression of SLC35B2 and NDST1.\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-30T08:44:15.198Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4bx7pyjms8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4bx7pyjms8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ningning","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32473007"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32272990"}],"categories":["Virus","Animal Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7t9snvwv3p","title":"Early fusion intermediate of ACE2-using coronavirus spike acting as antiviral target ","description":"Coronavirus fusion with and entry into the host cell depends on viral spike, which acts as a crucial component of viral infection. However, the lack of receptor-activated spike intermediate conformation has hindered a comprehensive understanding of spike-induced membrane fusion. Here, we captured an ACE2-induced early fusion intermediate conformation (E-FIC) of SARS-CoV-2 spike in which HR1 in S2 has ejected while S1 remains attached. This E-FIC can transition to the late fusion intermediate conformation after S2' cleavage. Leveraging this discovery, we designed an E-FIC-targeted dual-functional antiviral protein AL5E. AL5E effectively inactivated ACE2-using coronaviruses and inhibited their infection, outperforming a mono-functional antiviral in protecting animals against these coronaviruses. This study has identified the E-FIC and used it as target for the development of a dual-functional antiviral for the prevention and treatment of ACE2-using coronavirus infection. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-22T17:50:04.485Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7t9snvwv3p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7t9snvwv3p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lixiao","last_name":"Xing","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhimin","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinling","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qianying","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qiyu","last_name":"Mao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aihua","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuai","last_name":"Xia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zezhong","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lujia","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guangxu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenguo","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shibo","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lei","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Membrane Fusion","Viral Entry","Antiviral Agent Active against Respiratory Viruses","Coronavirus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"58gs4kwyct","title":"The essential role of heparan sulfate in the entry of PDCoV and other porcine coronaviruses","description":"Figure 1. The effect of heparin sodium treatment on PDCoV infection.\nFigure 2. Heparin sodium treatment inhibits infection by multiple coronaviruses. \nFigure 3. The effect of mitoxantrone treatment on PDCoV infection.\nFigure 4. The effects of genetic ablation of HS biosynthesis genes on PDCoV infection. \nFigure 5. SLC35B2, EXT1 and NDST1 HS biosynthesis genes are required for efficient infection by PEDV, SADS-CoV and TGEV.\nFigure 6. SLC35B2, EXT1 and NDST1 gene KO in LLC-PK1 cells reduces PDCoV attachment.\nFigure 7. Impact of the overexpression of HS biosynthesis genes on porcine coronavirus infection.\nFigure S1. Heparin sodium treatment showed no inhibitory effect on VSV infection.\nFigure S2. Mitoxantrone exhibits a stronger inhibitory effect on the viral entry stage than on post-entry stages. \nFigure S3. Effect of mitoxantrone treatment on PDCoV dsRNA formation.\nFigure S4. Mitoxantrone treatment inhibits infection by multiple coronaviruses. \nFigure S5. Mitoxantrone treatment of cells had no significant effect on VSV infection.\nFigure S6. Effect of genetic ablation of HS biosynthesis genes on HS expression. \nFigure S7. Overexpression of HS biosynthesis genes had no significant effect on VSV infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-26T15:54:19.456Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/58gs4kwyct","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/58gs4kwyct"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ningning","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32473007"},{"id":"https://ror.org/0426zh255","name":"China Postdoctoral Science Foundation","grant_id":"32272990"}],"categories":["Virus","Animal Virus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y872vkmbmj","title":"Host cell amplification of nutritional stress contributes to persistence in Chlamydia trachomatis","description":"Chlamydial starvation of distinct nutrients produce a superficially similar persistent state, implying convergence on a common intracellular environment. We employed host-pathogen dual RNA-sequencing under both iron- and tryptophan-starved conditions to systematically characterize the persistent chlamydial transcriptome and to define common contributions of the host cell transcriptional stress response in shaping the intracellular environment. The transcriptome of the infected host cells was highly specific to each nutritional stress, despite comparable effects on chlamydial growth and development in each condition. In contrast, the chlamydial transcriptomes between nutritional conditions were highly similar, suggesting some overlap in host cell responses to iron limitation and tryptophan starvation that contribute to a common persistent phenotype. We demonstrate that a commonality in the host cell responses is the suppression of guanosine triphosphate (GTP) biosynthesis, a nucleotide for which Chlamydia are auxotrophic. Host cell reduction in GTP levels amplifies the nutritional stress to intracellular chlamydiae in infection-relevant models of persistence, illustrating the determinative role the infected host cell plays in bacterial stress responses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-27T07:15:14.352Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y872vkmbmj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y872vkmbmj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rey","last_name":"Carabeo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nick","last_name":"Pokorzynski","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Monisha","last_name":"Alla","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chlamydia","Quantitative Reverse Transcription Polymerase Chain Reaction"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cnz8572h34","title":"Data For Effect of arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi and a root-associated fungus (Penicillium sp. EU0013) on the growth of tomato and lettuce","description":"This research constituted a series of studies seeking to uncover the spectrum of effects on host growth during dual inoculation of arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi and a root-associated fungus – Penicillium sp. EU0013 – which confers disease tolerance. Our findings showed that infection stress from these two groups of beneficial microbes could occur during dual inoculation leading to reduced growth, wilting of leaves and even mortality of the host. This is a rare report on fungus-fungus interaction in the literature on bio-inoculation, biofertilization, and plant-microbe interaction. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-15T02:19:31.124Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cnz8572h34","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cnz8572h34"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sarah","last_name":"Ibiang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Agricultural Science"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"km23bwyny6","title":"Identification of required host factors for SARS-CoV-2 infection in human cells, Daniloski et al","description":"Supplementary Tables S1 to S6 in MS Excel format for \"Identification of required host factors for SARS-CoV-2 infection in human cells\" by Daniloski*, Jordan* et al. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-21T14:00:41.151Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/km23bwyny6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/km23bwyny6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zharko","last_name":"Daniloski","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tristan","last_name":"Jordan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hans-Hermann","last_name":"Wessels","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daisy","last_name":"Hoagland","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Silva","last_name":"Kasela","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mateusz","last_name":"Legut","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Silas","last_name":"Maniatis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eleni","last_name":"Mimitou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Lu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Evan","last_name":"Geller","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Oded","last_name":"Danziger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brad","last_name":"Rosenberg","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hemali","last_name":"Phatnani","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Smibert","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tuuli","last_name":"Lappalainen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Benjamin","last_name":"tenOever","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Neville","last_name":"Sanjana","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Genomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xfkmxkcvzz","title":"Role of IL-22 during murine gammaherpesvirus infection","description":"Gammaherpesviruses are double-stranded DNA viruses that establish lifelong infections in over 95% of adults worldwide and are associated with multiple cancers and autoimmune disease. To persist within the host, these viruses exploit germinal center B cells to establish a long-lived latent reservoir. Although the cytokine IL-22 is best known for its protective role in bacterial and fungal infections, its contribution to gammaherpesvirus infection was unknown.  Using a murine model of gammaherpesvirus infection, we demonstrate that IL-22 promotes the establishment of chronic gammaherpesvirus infection without affecting acute viral replication. Mice lacking IL-22 exhibited reduced latent viral burden accompanied by impaired germinal center and plasma-cell responses along with diminished virus-specific and polyclonal antibody production. These findings suggest that IL-22 helps create an immune environment that favors viral latency. Our work identifies a previously unrecognized role for IL-22 in supporting chronic gammaherpesvirus infection and provides new insights into how host immune pathways contribute to lifelong gammaherpesvirus infection.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-15T12:16:25.226Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xfkmxkcvzz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xfkmxkcvzz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Christopher","last_name":"Jondle","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Virology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":140670,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":165,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":140670,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139940,"restricted":70,"embargoed":660},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2317},{